This would be a low-level wizard strategy guide ...
I play almost exclusively wizards, and they usually survive until
after the castle. But then...

(Off topic note: by the way, on YA(Crazy/Impossible)Conduct Idea : try
to ascend. I sometimes wonder if it is at all possible :P )

[snip part on my fond memories of early dead wizards]

The point with wizard is that you have powerful stuff, like scrolls,
potions, and mainly a wand and some mana. But that powerful stuff is
expendable, so you basically can't use it, except for some
emergencies. So don't use it! Mana recharges slooowly and on top of
that makes you hungry.

Usually, a young wizard is meant to hide behind his friend the
powerful warrior. The closest thing you have is a kitten. The primary
objective is to have a friendly (fast) large cat. Your number of hit
points is not really relevant since you will get beaten down if you
get in close quarters; the only requirement is to survive missile
weapons, which is done on lvls 2-3. (that's why the protection racket
is hard).

Now, in order to kill things yourself, drop your staff, pick up
daggers and throw them. They don't cost food or mana and can be
retreived. Only use spells when the enemy is actually a danger. Your
secondary objective is to raise your skill in dagger. Also, daggers
are a must against nymphs.

On intrinsics. Speed yourself up if you get the chance. Turn yourself
invisible if you can become visible again. Invisibility is a real boon
in the very early game. Even if you haven't yet any mean of turning
visible again, early invisibility may be worth it. Eat every poisonous
corpse until you feel healthy (though you should really feel stupid
:"Eech, this corpse was poisonous!" "Again ! You feel stupid!").
You'll eventually regain your lost abilities, and better increase your
strength after it won't go down anymore. If you have an early WoW,
blessed tin of green dragon meet (or green dragon scales), will save
your fully kitted character from a poisonous spike. Oh, and eating
floating eyes is a must do.

On armor. You will slowly but surely accumulate pieces (leather body
armor, hat, boots, gloves, small shield) that will in the end get you
below zero. Crystal plates are way too heavy (especially when on a
poisonous diet), as are other heavy armor (which also prevent spell
casting). Better unburdened AC 5 then burdened AC 1. When you're
around -2, you are set. Don't forget protection if you have the money.

On emergencies. Okay, so you've fallen through that trap door ? Three
rothes are coming your way ? That's when your wand of
lightning/cold/sleeping/teleportation will perhaps save you. The goal
is to quickly find the upstairs.

On pets. 2 pets are better than 1. 3 can be bothersome. Note that
unicorns, horses, cats and dogs can almost always be at least
pacified.

On wizard's wisdom. If you're spoiled, wizard have the easiest time
identifiying stuff, as they don't start from scratch (already some
potions, rings, scrolls, and wands are identified). So you'll likely
be able to add quickly to your emergency arsenal.

Finally, once you're set, your large cat will look wimpy to you.
However it will still b/u/c-id things and eat certain corpses for you.

Well that was it. And keep your cloak!

